  "body": "The way in which load events are fired for frames and iframes is done via action at a distance\r\n\r\n> When a Document in a frame is marked as completely loaded, the user agent must queue a task to fire an event named load at the frame element.\r\n\r\n> When a Document in an iframe is marked as completely loaded, the user agent must run the iframe load event steps.\r\n\r\nThis would be better done by actually running these appropriate steps when you mark the document as completely loaded.\r\n\r\nAs far as I can tell these are the only locations that do so and would need to be updated:\r\n\r\n- https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#documents:completely-loaded\r\n- https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#windows:completely-loaded\r\n\r\nThe first of these two says\r\n\r\n> When a Document is created by a script using the createDocument() or createHTMLDocument() the Document is both ready for post-load tasks and completely loaded immediately.\r\n\r\nwhich is essentially monkey-patching the createDocument()/createHTMLDocument() methods (and appears to miss the Document() constructor---not sure if intentional). Ideally it should be moved to DOM's definition of these methods. Perhaps as a callout like \"Run the [synthetic document setup steps]\" where \"synthetic document setup steps\" is defined in HTML.",
